---
// The finding of the piece: sort the same 819 months by INFLATION instead of by
// yield direction and the record splits cleanly. With trailing inflation under
// 4%, the next twelve months beat inflation roughly three times in four
// whichever way yields had moved; at 4% or above, both directions collapse
// towards a coin flip. The columns decide, the rows barely matter.
//
// Real (after-inflation) basis throughout, because that is where the damage
// actually shows: hot-inflation months often still LOOK fine in nominal terms.
//
// Encoding per the house colour rule: the four condition cells get
// ONE neutral tint — no green/red verdicts, the numbers carry the story. The
// small row swatches reuse the gold/jade identity established by the exhibit
// above (gold = after a yield rise, jade = after a fall).
//
// The 2x2 stays a 2x2 on a phone: at 390px each cell is ~150px wide, which
// holds a two-digit mono figure and two short lines comfortably. Verified in
// the emulated viewport rather than assumed.
import ToolBrand from '../ToolBrand.astro';

const CELLS = [
  { dir: 'up', infl: 'cool', n: 263, positive: 74, median: 8.7 },
  { dir: 'up', infl: 'hot', n: 171, positive: 56, median: 4.4 },
  { dir: 'down', infl: 'cool', n: 314, positive: 81, median: 13.5 },
  { dir: 'down', infl: 'hot', n: 67, positive: 54, median: 5.2 },
];
const cell = (dir, infl) => CELLS.find((c) => c.dir === dir && c.infl === infl);
---

<div class="is2">
  <div class="is2-head">
    <span class="is2-lbl">It was the inflation, not the rates</span>
    <span class="is2-sub">Same 819 months, next-12-month returns <em>after inflation</em>, sorted both ways</span>
  </div>

  <div class="is2-body">
    <div class="is2-grid" role="table" aria-label="Real 12-month share returns split by yield direction and trailing inflation">
      <div class="is2-corner" role="columnheader"></div>
      <div class="is2-col" role="columnheader">inflation<br /><b>under 4%</b></div>
      <div class="is2-col" role="columnheader">inflation<br /><b>4% or higher</b></div>

      {['up', 'down'].map((dir) => (
        <>
          <div class="is2-row" role="rowheader">
            <i class={`is2-sw ${dir}`}></i>
            <span>10-year <b>yield {dir === 'up' ? 'higher' : 'lower'}</b> than a year earlier</span>
          </div>
          {['cool', 'hot'].map((infl) => {
            const c = cell(dir, infl);
            return (
              <div class="is2-cell" role="cell">
                <span class="is2-big">{c.positive}<i>/100</i></span>
                <span class="is2-what">beat inflation</span>
                <span class="is2-med">median {c.median >= 0 ? '+' : ''}{c.median}% real &middot; {c.n} mo</span>
              </div>
            );
          })}
        </>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="is2-note-inline">
      Moving the line to 3% or to 5% changes the sizes, not the shape: the cool cells
      stay near three-in-four, the hot cells stay near a coin flip. And the hot cells
      often still looked respectable <em>before</em> inflation &mdash; a median 13.8%
      nominal in the rising-yield one &mdash; which is exactly how purchasing power
      slips away without a headline.
    </p>

    <p class="is2-now">
      Where is now? US inflation printed <b>3.4%</b> for July 2026 &mdash; the left
      column, with the dividing line close enough to watch.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="is2-out">
    <p class="is2-out-s">
      With inflation <b>under 4%</b>, the year after a yield rise beat inflation
      <b> 74 times in 100</b>. At <b>4% or above</b>, it was <b>56</b> &mdash; and
      falling yields did not save you either, at 54. Read the column, not the row.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="is2-notes">
    <p>
      Trailing inflation is the prior 12 months of US CPI; real returns deflate the
      next 12 months of Shiller total return by the CPI over the same window. FRED
      GS10 for yields. Apr 1954 &ndash; Jun 2022 starts, overlapping windows. The July
      2026 CPI reading is the US Bureau of Labor Statistics release of 12 Aug 2026.
      Our computation.
    </p>
    <ToolBrand />
  </div>
</div>

<style is:global>
.is2{
  --is2-bd:var(--color-border); --is2-t1:var(--color-dark); --is2-t2:var(--color-body);
  --is2-deep:var(--color-navy);
  --is2-mono:var(--font-mono),'SF Mono',Menlo,Consolas,monospace;
  background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--is2-bd);border-radius:12px;
  box-shadow:0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,.05),0 2px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.04);
  overflow:hidden;margin:26px 0;color:var(--is2-t1)
}
.is2-head{padding:14px 20px;background:var(--color-surface);border-bottom:1px solid var(--is2-bd)}
.is2-lbl{display:block;font-size:14.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.1px;color:var(--is2-t1)}
.is2-sub{display:block;margin-top:4px;font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;
  text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.8px;color:var(--is2-t2)}
.is2-body{padding:16px 20px 8px}
.is2-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(96px,1.2fr) 1fr 1fr;gap:7px}
.is2-col{align-self:end;font-size:12px;line-height:1.35;color:var(--is2-t2);
  text-align:center;padding-bottom:2px}
.is2-col b{color:var(--is2-t1)}
.is2-row{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.35;
  color:var(--is2-t2);padding-right:2px}
.is2-row b{color:var(--is2-t1)}
.is2-sw{width:11px;height:11px;border-radius:3px;flex:none;display:inline-block}
.is2-sw.up{background:var(--color-gold)}
.is2-sw.down{background:var(--color-brand-700)}
/* One neutral tint for all four condition cells — the numbers carry the story. */
.is2-cell{background:var(--color-surface);border:1px solid var(--is2-bd);border-radius:8px;
  padding:11px 8px 10px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:2px;
  text-align:center}
.is2-big{font-family:var(--is2-mono);font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:var(--is2-t1);
  font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;line-height:1.1}
.is2-big i{font-style:normal;font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--color-muted)}
.is2-what{font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.7px;
  color:var(--is2-t2)}
.is2-med{font-family:var(--is2-mono);font-size:11px;color:var(--color-muted);
  font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.post-content .is2-note-inline{margin:14px 0 0;font-size:12px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--is2-t2)}
.post-content .is2-now{margin:10px 0 6px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--is2-t2);
  padding:10px 13px;background:var(--color-surface);border-left:3px solid var(--color-brand-700);
  border-radius:0 6px 6px 0}
.is2-now b{font-family:var(--is2-mono);color:var(--color-brand-700)}
.is2-out{background:var(--is2-deep);color:#fff;padding:17px 20px}
.post-content .is2-out-s{font-size:15px;line-height:1.62;color:#D8DEE8;margin:0}
.is2-out-s b{font-family:var(--is2-mono);font-weight:600;color:#fff}
.is2-notes{padding:14px 20px 15px;border-top:1px solid var(--is2-bd);background:var(--color-surface)}
.post-content .is2-notes p{font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--color-muted);margin:0 0 8px}
@media (max-width:420px){
  .is2-grid{grid-template-columns:minmax(84px,1fr) 1fr 1fr;gap:5px}
  .is2-big{font-size:20px}
  .is2-row{font-size:11.5px}
}
</style>
